<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">314 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Luceria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucera, ancient Luceria, is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on a hill overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tavoliere-delle-puglie">Tavoliere</a> plain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony there in 314 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a>. In the 1220s Emperor Frederick II settled Muslims from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the town, which remained a Muslim community until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles II of Naples</a> destroyed it in 1300. Its great Angevin fortress still stands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucera.jpg" alt="Lucera" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vonvikken · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_comune_of_Lucera_(province_of_Foggia,_region_Apulia,_Italy).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lucera stands on a flat knoll in the Tavoliere Plains, near the foot of the Daunian Mountains, in the province of Foggia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. It lies in the territory of the ancient tribe of the Daunii, and excavations show a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> village within the present city boundaries. The origin of the name is uncertain: it may come from Lucius, a mythical Daunian king, from a temple of the goddess Lux Cereris, or from Etruscan words meaning holy wood. From 1579 until 1806 Lucera was the capital of the Province of Capitanata and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molise">Molise</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 321 BCE the Roman army, deceived into thinking Lucera was besieged by the Samnites, walked into an ambush and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-caudine-forks">Battle of the Caudine Forks</a>. The Samnites occupied the city but were thrown out by a revolt, and Lucera sought Roman protection.</p><p>About 2,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> moved there to strengthen the ties, and the city became known as a steadfast supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. During the late Republic Pompey set up his headquarters there but left when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> approached, and Caesar's clemency spared the city. After the war between Octavian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, Octavian settled many veterans on its lands, and the Roman amphitheatre dates from this Augustan period. In 663 the Eastern Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> captured the city from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> and destroyed it.</p><p>In 1224 Emperor Frederick II, responding to religious uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, expelled the Muslims from the island and moved many to Lucera over the next two decades. Under royal control they paid taxes and gave military service, and their numbers reached between 15,000 and 20,000. The city was called Lucaera Saracenorum as the last stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and in peacetime the Muslims were mainly farmers.</p><p>The colony lasted 75 years until Christian forces under Giovanni Pippino di Barletta sacked it in 1300 with the acquiescence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles II of Naples</a>. Most inhabitants were killed or, in nearly 10,000 cases, sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The mosques were demolished and churches built in their place, and Charles II settled Christians, chiefly Burgundian and Provençal soldiers and farmers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lucera is the seat of the Diocese of Lucera-Troia. Its cathedral, built in 1300 on the site of the last standing medieval mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, is among its sights together with the medieval Lucera Castle. The Roman amphitheatre, discovered in 1932, measures about 131 by 99 metres and could hold 16,000 to 18,000 spectators. Descendants of Provençal colonists still speak a Franco-Provençal dialect in the villages of Faeto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a> di San Vito.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick II deported about 60,000 Muslims to <b>Lucera</b> in Apulia from 1223, many of them enslaved, and the removal of Islam was complete by the late 1240s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the twelfth and thirteenth centuries Latin Christianity and feudal lordship gradually displaced the Muslim communities, and after revolts Frederick II deported the remaining Muslims of Sicily to <b>Lucera</b> in Apulia in the 1220s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Samnite Wars the Daunian towns sided with Rome; Livy records alliances in 318 BCE, and Rome founded <b>Luceria</b> in 314 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a></li></ul></section>
